			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4849371">
									

<p>Summoned via PM.</p>
<p>The premise here is nice - whilst not groundbreaking it's a lovely little idea and I can see fun things being done with it.</p>
<p>My issue is that the narrative involves a set-up, repetition to cement the ideas, and then nothing. Like <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/cybersqyd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5336470); return false;"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=5336470&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608465202" alt="cybersqyd" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=5336470)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/cybersqyd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5336470); return false;">cybersqyd</a></span> said, there's not much that actually happens in the narrative. It's all set-up and no pay-off. You really need more to actually happen here, a clearer throughline, that will carry the reader from the beginning to the end and make the story feel satisfying. If nothing happens, it will end up feeling flat and unengaging.</p>
<p>Again, similarly to <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/cybersqyd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5336470); return false;"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=5336470&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608465202" alt="cybersqyd" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=5336470)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/cybersqyd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5336470); return false;">cybersqyd</a></span>, I feel like there are two competing narratives here that aren't going to be able to naturally co-exist. This would be a far stronger pitch if it focused on a more personal, emotionally-driven, and intimate story like sqyd suggested. Ver suggestion about a D-class centric narrative is a solid start, and I would recommend pursuing something akin to that. With these two competing narrative ideas pulling the article in different directions, it will inevitably end up feeling confused, so you really need to pick which one you want to write.</p>
<p>I'm afraid I don't have much to add here, but I hope this is useful to you nonetheless. Good luck!</p>

