														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4849349">
									

<p>Hello aleph_null99! lets take a look :)</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><strong>The concept:</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Elevator Pitch: This SCP is a gramaphone album that when played will progress the player into dementia if listened to. It has six stages, and each time a song passes the dementia progresses further. For example, a person listening to Stage 1 might forget where his keycard is, and somebody in stage 4 would forget their address or phone number.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This wouldn't work as is, the concept isn't any different from the source material and it doesn't really show anything new or build on the original idea. Currently, it just feels like a quick way of adding the album onto the main-site.</p>
<p>That said, the concept itself (if it were to be an article) boils down to a &quot;thing that does a thing&quot;, as the only aspects of the skip shown here are the effects. Whats its purpose? why does it have its anomaly? how does that anomaly affect the people around it? why should the concept as a whole make me as the reader invested? what separates this from the original?- would it be able to stand on its own, without any immediate ties to the source material?</p>
<p><strong>The narrative:</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Central Narrative: A woman finds this in her house during a garage sale and listens to the entire album. It culminates to an MTF entering her house in the middle of her going through shock and being escorted to the mtf armoured van with the anomaly and her going into a coma on the way to the next site.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>How does this end up in an MTF team raiding her house? how does the Foundation get involved to begin with? who is this woman?- what about her will make me invested? why does she go into shock? what happens to the skip itself?-is it tested? does it affect anyone else?</p>
<p>Currently the narrative is fairly vague, as it doesn't really give any insight into what the story will look like as a draft. The Foundation isn't really given a clear introduction, as I'm not sure how they got involved, or what they do with the skip- whats the payoff?. Along with this, the character isn't really established, which makes it hard to get invested.</p>
<p><strong>Hook:</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Hook/Attention Grabber: Pending review for the women to gain access to SCP-407.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This isn't really related to the rest of the skip, as its not mentioned anywhere else and doesn't play any real part in the concept or narrative. This feels closer to additional info than anything.- for the hook, try putting in the main idea and/or conflict of the narrative and/or concept.</p>
<p>I wouldn't recommend cross linking series one articles, as it takes focus away from the concept and can very easily turn the article into &quot;what would happen if [insert SCP here] and [insert SCP here] interacted?&quot;. If you're interested in cross-linking, I would highly recommend looking at later series (such as series 5 and 6) rather than series 1.</p>
<p><strong>Red or green?</strong><br />
Due to my afore mentioned concerns, no-greenlight.</p>
<p>If you would like me review this (or any concept) further, then please feel free to send me a PM or ask via IRC. Response times generally range from 2-12 hours over PMs. If your looking for additional critiques in general, then I would highly recommend contacting the butterfly squad :)</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Cheers,</p>
<p>-RED</p>

