			<div class="content" id="post-content-4849297">
									

<p>I'm not exactly sure how the central narrative ties into the core anomaly: I assume that it means that while the book can kill anyone who reads it, it doesn't always choose to do so?</p>
<p>Beyond this, I'm not exactly sure what the central narrative is about, if you catch my drift: there's vague allusions to the individuals maybe knowing each other, and there being trials they face, but like&#8230;where does it all go? What's the big hook that like, engages a reader into caring about these people and the outcome of their trials? What actually happens during the trials? What are their characters and morals like?</p>
<p>My concern is that the outcome of this (both the characters dying) feels very obviously like it's going to happen: the description will almost certainly setup the lethality of this (if the ConProcs don't), and so it feels like the overall arc will be very obvious and then hard to get a reader to engage? I also feel like a generic item which judges the sins of someone is&#8230;not hugely interesting alone: it's something which is proposed a lot and almost never goes anywhere because it's just, not very interesting on it's own.</p>

							</div>

								
					
